# The cspell dictionary is one alphabetically sorted word per line, so two
# branches that each add a word collide at the same line by construction — a
# conflict whose only correct resolution is "keep both sides". `merge=union`
# is git's built-in driver for exactly that: it takes both sides' lines
# instead of raising a conflict. It needs no local configuration, so it
# applies to every merge and rebase, in any clone.
#
# Union merge does not sort or de-duplicate, so it can leave the file unsorted
# and with a word twice. Both are self-healing: the `file-contents-sorter` in
# cfg/pre-commit-lint.yml runs with `--unique`, so the next commit touching
# the file re-sorts and de-duplicates it, and neither state breaks anything
# before then — cspell tolerates both, and the dictionary-hygiene check reads
# the file into a set.
#
# Do NOT extend this to a file where a dropped or doubled line changes
# meaning. It is sound here only because the dictionary is an unordered set
# that happens to be stored sorted.
cfg/dictionary.txt merge=union
